!! `search_lines` MCP Tool

Per-line search across tiddler fields. Returns one entry per matching line, with optional context lines around each match. The underlying engine is shared with the [[search-lines Operator]]; this page documents the MCP tool wrapper.

!! Parameters

|!Parameter |!Default |!Description |
|`pattern` |required |string to find (or regex when `regexp=true`) |
|`fields` |`["text"]` |array of field names to scan |
|`case_sensitive` |`false` |case-sensitive match |
|`regexp` |`false` |treat `pattern` as a JS regex |
|`words` |`false` |wrap pattern with `\b` word boundaries |
|`invert` |`false` |return lines that do NOT match |
|`context` |`0` |context lines around each match (symmetric) |
|`context_before` |inherits `context` |lines before each match |
|`context_after` |inherits `context` |lines after each match |
|`filter` |`[all[tiddlers]!is[system]]` |TW filter expression to scope the search |
|`include_system` |`false` |convenience flag for $:/ tiddlers; ignored if `filter` is set |

!! Output format

One line per emitted match or context line:

```
<title>:<field>:L<line>: <line text>
<title>:<field>:c<line>: <line text>
```

`L` marks a match line, `c` marks a context line.

When context windows from adjacent matches overlap or sit within one line of each other, they merge so each line is emitted at most once.

''Multi-field array values'' (e.g. `tags`, `list`) are serialised via `$tw.utils.stringifyList` before matching, so the on-disk .tid form is what gets scanned (multi-word entries wrap in `[[...]]`). Other non-string field values are skipped silently.

!! Bracket-clash advantage

The MCP tool invokes the operator function directly, sidestepping the TW filter-syntax limitation where `]` inside an operand terminates parsing. So `search_lines` (and `replace_in_tiddlers`) handle `]`-bearing regexes that the filter-expression form cannot. See [[search-lines Operator]] for the wikitext-side workaround.

!! Example prompts

These are what you type to an AI client; the AI dispatches the appropriate `search_lines` call.

* "Find every line mentioning 'MCP' in any tiddler"
* "Find tiddlers tagged Server that mention 'transclude', with 2 lines of context"
* "Search captions for the regex `\[\[[^|]+\|.*\]\]`" — uses `regexp=true`, `fields=["caption"]`. The `]` in the pattern is fine here; the bracket-clash only affects the wikitext filter form.
* "List lines in Best Practices that do NOT start with a `!!` heading" — uses `invert=true`, `filter="[title[Best Practices]]"`

!! Direct invocations

Each row mirrors a row in the parameters table. Test against the running wiki with any MCP client (e.g. an inline tool call from Claude Code).

```
search_lines(pattern="MCP")
search_lines(pattern="MCP", fields=["text", "caption"])
search_lines(pattern="MCP", words=true)
search_lines(pattern="MCP", case_sensitive=true)
search_lines(pattern="search.lines", regexp=true)
search_lines(pattern="MCP", invert=true, filter="[tag[Server]]")
search_lines(pattern="transclude", context=2)
search_lines(pattern="transclude", context_before=1, context_after=3)
search_lines(pattern="token", filter="[tag[Server]]")
search_lines(pattern="[A-Z]+_tiddler", regexp=true)
```

The last row demonstrates the MCP advantage: a `]`-bearing regex pattern works here but not in `[search-lines:regexp[...]]` (see [[search-lines Operator]] for the wikitext-side workaround).

